 	<TEXT>
 		<Tag>TXT_KEY_UNIT_KINGDOM_OF_BENIN_DISCIPLINED_CROSSBOWMAN_PEDIA</Tag>
 		<English>[TAB]A crossbow is a ranged weapon using an elastic launching device consisting of a bow-like assembly called a prod, mounted horizontally on a main frame called a tiller, which is hand-held in a similar fashion to the stock of a long gun. Crossbows shoot arrow-like projectiles called bolts or quarrels. A person who shoots crossbow is called a crossbowman, an arbalister or an arbalist (after the arbalest, a European crossbow variant used during the 12th century).[PARAGRAPH:1][TAB]On top of the usual info that can be found on the [LINK=UNIT_CROSSBOWMAN]Crossbowman's page[\LINK], military operations in the Kingdom Of Benin relied on a well trained disciplined force. Formations were broken down into sub-units under designated commanders. Foreign observers often commented favorably on Benin's discipline and organization as "better disciplined than any other Guinea nation", contrasting them with the slacker troops from the Gold Coast.[PARAGRAPH:1][TAB]Before the coming of the gun, guilds of blacksmiths were charged with war production-particularly swords and iron spearheads. In addition, crossbowmen formed a specialized unit of the Benin army. Archers and crossbowmen were trained in target and field archery.Benin's tactics were well organized, with preliminary plans weighed by the Oba and his sub-commanders. Logistics were organized to support missions from the usual porter forces, water transport via canoe, and requisitioning from localities the army passed through. Movement of troops via canoes was critically important in the lagoons, creeks and rivers of the Niger Delta, a key area of Benin's domination.</English>

